//! The Array module: a **fixed-capacity** growable array — a count plus storage, with push and pop.
//!
//! Written in Jairs like the rest of the standard library. The fourth module, and the first that is a *data
//! structure* rather than a set of operations on something the language already has.
//!
//! # Read this first: the type is generic; the operations are intentionally concrete
//!
//! `Array :: struct($T)` crosses a module boundary (ADR-0117), so callers may declare `Array(s64)` or another
//! instance. The procedures below still operate on `Array(s64)`: an imported polymorphic procedure cannot yet
//! be instantiated across the boundary (E0268), and publishing a generic-looking routine that no importer can
//! call would be worse than naming the supported instance honestly.
//!
//! This remains a fixed-capacity container by design. The native `[..]T` plus `modules/List` is the growable
//! shape; `Array` is useful when capacity belongs in the value's static layout and no allocation or cleanup is
//! wanted.
//!
//! # Why the capacity is in the struct
//!
//! `Array(s64, 16)` would be the natural spelling, and a parameterised struct takes **type** arguments only
//! (ADR-0085 §3). A `$N` on the *procedures* does not help, because the capacity has to be part of the
//! **struct's** type — that is what makes two arrays of different capacity different types with different
//! layouts. So the capacity is baked into the declaration below, and a caller wanting another one declares
//! their own struct with the same shape. That is a real limitation; naming it here is better than letting a
//! caller find it.

/// A count and its storage: the array's used prefix is `items[0 .. count)`.
///
/// The capacity is 16 because it has to be *something*: even with a parameterised struct the arguments are
/// **types** (ADR-0085 §3), so a capacity cannot come from a caller at all. Sixteen is enough to fill an array
/// in a test without making the struct large.
Array :: struct($T) {
  /// The storage. Only the first `count` elements are meaningful; the rest hold what the declaration zeroed
  /// them to, which is a real value rather than garbage (ADR-0039's explicit `zero`) — and therefore
  /// indistinguishable from a genuine element, which is why `count` is the boundary every routine respects.
  items: [16]T;
  /// How many elements are in use. Never greater than [`CAPACITY`], which every routine here maintains.
  count: s64;
}

/// The capacity of every `Array(T)`, as a constant a caller can compare against.
///
/// Exported so a caller can ask "will this fit" without hard-coding 16 — the number appears once here and
/// nowhere else, so changing it cannot leave a stale bound behind in a caller.
CAPACITY :: 16;

/// Appends `v`, answering `false` when the array is full.
///
/// **`false` rather than a trap**, unlike an out-of-range index (ADR-0003). The difference is who made the
/// mistake: indexing past a bound the compiler knows is a *program* error, while filling a fixed-capacity
/// buffer is an ordinary thing a correct program does and then has to handle. A trap would make every caller
/// pre-check the count, which is the check this routine already does.
///
/// Takes a pointer because it mutates: a by-value struct parameter would append to a copy, and the caller would
/// see nothing — the kind of silent no-op a `bool` return cannot warn about.
push :: (a: *Array(s64), v: s64) -> bool {
  if a.count >= CAPACITY {
    return false;
  }
  a.items[a.count] = v;
  a.count = a.count + 1;
  return true;
}

/// Removes and returns the last element, plus whether there was one.
///
/// Two return values (ADR-0008) rather than a sentinel, because **every** `s64` is a legitimate element — there
/// is no value that could mean "empty" without excluding it from the array. That is the opposite call from
/// `String.find`, which returns `-1`, and the difference is exactly this: an index has values outside its
/// domain, an element does not.
///
/// The element is left in `items`, not cleared. Nothing can read it — `count` is the boundary and every routine
/// here respects it — and clearing would need a zero value the element type may not have.
pop :: (a: *Array(s64)) -> (s64, bool) {
  if a.count == 0 {
    return 0, false;
  }
  a.count = a.count - 1;
  return a.items[a.count], true;
}

/// The element at `index`, plus whether the index was in the **used** range.
///
/// "In range" means `0 <= index < count`, not `< CAPACITY`: reading an unused slot would return the value the
/// declaration zeroed it to, which is a real number and therefore indistinguishable from a genuine element.
/// That is the well-typed-placeholder failure one level up from the compiler, and refusing it here is what
/// keeps `count` meaningful.
get :: (a: *Array(s64), index: s64) -> (s64, bool) {
  if index < 0 {
    return 0, false;
  }
  if index >= a.count {
    return 0, false;
  }
  return a.items[index], true;
}

/// Replaces the element at `index`, answering `false` when the index is outside the used range.
///
/// It will not *extend* the array — `set(a, a.count, v)` is `false`, not a push. Growing through an assignment
/// would make the array's length depend on which indices a caller happened to touch, and `push` is the routine
/// that has something to say about capacity.
set :: (a: *Array(s64), index: s64, v: s64) -> bool {
  if index < 0 {
    return false;
  }
  if index >= a.count {
    return false;
  }
  a.items[index] = v;
  return true;
}

/// Empties the array.
///
/// Setting `count` to zero is the whole operation, because the storage holds no resources: an element is a
/// value, and there is nothing to release. When element types can own memory this becomes a real question, and
/// answering it then is better than pretending now.
clear :: (a: *Array(s64)) {
  a.count = 0;
}

/// Whether the array holds nothing.
is_empty :: (a: *Array(s64)) -> bool {
  return a.count == 0;
}

/// Whether another `push` would fail.
///
/// Named rather than left to `a.count == CAPACITY`, so a caller checking before a loop reads as intent — and so
/// the comparison lives in one place if the capacity ever stops being a constant.
is_full :: (a: *Array(s64)) -> bool {
  return a.count >= CAPACITY;
}
